{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And to every beast of the earth, etc.</b> The verse juxtaposed 'beast of the earth' with what was stated just before it, ״לָכֶם יִהְיֶה לְאָכְלָה״ ('for you it shall be for food') etc., so that it appears He gives human food to the beast equally. And He finished saying <b>for food</b> a second time adjacent to 'green herbage,' so that it appears that the food of beast and bird is only from the grass of the field. The intention is that HaShem did not create for beast, living creature, and bird anything other than specifically green herbage. And perhaps you will say: if there is no green herbage, the living creature is found dead — Scripture comes to teach: ״לָכֶם לְאָכְלָה וּלְכָל חַיַּת הָאָרֶץ״ ('for you for food and for every beast of the earth') etc. We thus say that man lives from the produce of the earth and from the fruit of the tree, and the living creature and the bird from the grasses and the herbage; and if there is no sustenance from these, then their sustenance is from human food. And one may hint at this in His saying (Deuteronomy 11:15): ״וְנָתַתִּי עֵשֶׂב בְּשָׂדְךָ לִבְהֶמְתֶּךָ״ ('and I will give grass in your field for your cattle'), and with this ״וְאָכַלְתָּ וְשָׂבָעְתָּ״ ('and you shall eat and be satisfied'); but without this, satisfaction will be lacking, for the living creature and the bird will eat it, since their provisions are your provisions."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:57:44.708785081Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}